course: english
lesson: 21
title: Author's choices: why this word, why this way
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Authors choose every word on purpose. «The wind screamed through the trees» is not the same as «the wind blew». Screamed makes the wind sound wild and frightening, like a person in terror. To answer a «why did the author…» question, name the choice, then say what it makes you feel or picture.
explain: Shape is a choice too. A chapter that is one word long, «Silence.», is not lazy: it stops you dead, and you feel the quiet. Repeating «closer… closer… closer» slows time down and builds suspense as the danger comes nearer. Italics, the slanted letters in «*Don't look back,* she told herself», often show a character's thoughts.
explain: The voice tells you who someone is. Mark in Bristol says, «Proper job, the writer made the baddie talk all posh. Why's that, my lover?» («proper job» is West Country for well done, and «my lover» is just a friendly way to say mate.) The posh voice can make the baddie seem cold and powerful. A story told by a dog is not dumb: it shows the world from a new viewpoint.
explain: Aanya in Singapore asks, «Teacher, why author write like that one, so many "and then, and then"?» («like that one» is Singapore English for «in that way».) Sometimes it is on purpose: «and then, and then» can make a narrator sound young and breathless. Sound words like «crash, bang, wallop» let you hear the action, and a metaphor like «the house was a sleeping giant» makes the house seem huge, still and maybe dangerous if it wakes.
fact: An author's choice is a decision about words, sentences or layout, made on purpose.
fact: The effect is what a choice makes the reader feel, picture or think.
fact: Strong verbs like «screamed» or «crept» create a stronger picture than plain ones like «blew» or «went».
fact: Very short sentences or paragraphs can build tension or make a moment stand out.
fact: Repetition can build suspense and make time feel slow.
fact: Italics can show thoughts, a word said with emphasis, or a word from another language.
fact: An opening hook, like «It was the worst day of my life», makes the reader want to know more.
fact: Headings and bullet points in non-fiction make facts quick to find and easy to read.
fact: The narrator is who tells the story, and it can be an animal or an object as well as a person.
fact: Onomatopoeia means words that sound like their meaning, like crash, bang, buzz and sizzle.
fact: A metaphor says one thing is another, without «like» or «as»: the house was a sleeping giant.
fact: The way a character speaks, posh, rough, local or old-fashioned, helps to show what they are like.
fact: When you explain a choice, saying «to make it more interesting» is too vague; name the exact effect.
exercise: «The wind screamed through the trees.» Why did the author use screamed instead of blew? | To make the wind sound wild and frightening | Because blew is a word children are not allowed to use | To make the story longer | Because the trees were people
exercise: A chapter is just one word: «Silence.» Jamie says the author was lazy. What is a better view? | It makes the reader stop and feel the quiet | The author ran out of ideas and wanted to get the book finished quickly | It saves paper | It's a mistake by the printer
exercise: Why is «*Don't look back,* she told herself» written in italics? | To show her thoughts | Because the printer's machine ran out of normal straight letters halfway through | To show she is shouting | To show it's a mistake
exercise: Why might an author write «closer… closer… closer»? | To build suspense as the danger comes nearer | To make the book reach the right number of pages for the publisher | Because the author forgot | To teach counting
exercise: The writer makes the baddie talk all posh. What could this show? | That the baddie is cold, proud and powerful | That all posh people are baddies | Nothing, voices in books mean nothing at all, they are just there to fill the page | That the baddie is a child
exercise: A story begins: «It was the worst day of my life.» Why start like this? | To hook the reader into wanting to know what happened | Because the author wanted to tell the whole ending first so that there are no surprises later | To be polite | Because all stories start this way
exercise: Why has a non-fiction author used bullet points? | To make facts quick to find | To make the page look pretty | Because sentences are not allowed in non-fiction books for children | To fill space
exercise: A story is told by the dog. Ethan says, «Dogs can't talk, that's dumb.» Why might the author choose a dog narrator? | To show the world from a new point of view | Because the author did not know that real dogs cannot talk | To make it a true story | Because people are boring
exercise: Aanya asks why the author writes «and then, and then». What could the reason be? | To make the narrator sound young or breathless | Because the author doesn't know any other joining words at all | Because «and then» is the only correct way | To make it rhyme
exercise: A poem says «crash, bang, wallop». What is the effect? | You can hear the noisy action | It makes the poem quieter and calmer, like a lullaby before bed | It shows the poet is angry | It teaches spelling
exercise: The author calls the house «a sleeping giant». What does this suggest? | It is huge and still, and might seem dangerous | It is an actual giant lying asleep in a field somewhere near the town | It is small and cosy | It is falling down
exercise: What is onomatopoeia? | Words that sound like their meaning | Words that mean the opposite of each other, like hot and cold or big and small | Long words | Rhyming words
exercise: Which is a metaphor? | The house was a sleeping giant | The house was as big as a giant | The house was big | The giant lived in a house
exercise: Mark says «Proper job.» What does it mean in the West Country? | Well done | A real job | A hard job | Get a job
exercise: Which explanation of «The wind screamed» gets more marks? | It makes the storm sound terrifying, like a person screaming | It makes it more interesting | It's a good word | The author likes the wind
exercise: Aanya says «like that one». What does she mean? | In that way | Like that book | The first one | I like that
test: «the author wrote 'Silence.' as a whole chapter cos he was being lazy lol». What's the best reply? | It's on purpose, to make the moment stand out | Yes, authors get paid by the word | Yes, he forgot the rest of the chapter | No, it's because silence is a long word
test: ★★ Which sentence makes the cat sound most secret and careful? | The cat crept across the dark kitchen | The cat went into the kitchen, which was quite dark and had a fridge in the corner | The cat was in the kitchen | The cat ran to the kitchen
test: ★★ An author writes: «Run. Hide. Now.» Why use three very short sentences? | To make it feel urgent and fast | To save ink, because ink costs a lot of money for the people who print books | Because the author can't write long sentences | To make it calm
test: ★★★ Two versions: A) «Mia opened the box.» B) «Slowly, slowly, Mia lifted the lid.» Which builds more suspense, and why? | B, the repetition slows the moment down | A, short sentences are always more exciting than any other kind | B, because it is longer | A, it has fewer words
test: ★★★ In a story, a character always speaks in long, fancy words, like «I shall partake of my luncheon.» What is the author most likely showing? | The character is posh or old-fashioned | The author swallowed a dictionary | The character can't speak English | The character is a baby
test: In a cartoon, every loud noise appears in big letters: BOOM! ZAP! What is this device in writing? | Onomatopoeia | A metaphor | A simile, because the noise is compared to something using the word like | A heading
test: Ethan: «A dog telling the story is dumb, dogs can't talk.» What is true? | Narrators can be animals, and it gives a new viewpoint | Ethan is right, only people can narrate | Dog narrators are only for babies | Stories must be told by the author's mum or dad, who can check that it is all true
test: What would happen to a non-fiction page about volcanoes if all the headings and bullet points were removed? | Facts would be harder to find | The facts would become untrue | It would be easier to read | Nothing, layout never matters
test: «why's the writer say 'the sea swallowed the boat', seas don't eat». What is the effect? | It makes the sea seem hungry and powerful | It's a mistake | It means the boat was food | The writer thinks the sea really eats things, like a whale or a shark does
test: Teacher: «Why use "crept" not "walked"?» Liam: «Cos it's better.» How can he improve the answer? | Say the effect: it sounds quiet and sneaky | Write «because it's much, much better» | Use a longer word | Give up and write «I don't know» because there's never a single right answer
exam: Identify the principal effect achieved by the author's selection of the verb «screamed» in the sentence «The wind screamed through the trees». | The wind is rendered wild and frightening | The wind is rendered gentle | The sentence is lengthened | The reader is informed of the precise speed of the wind, measured in kilometres per hour
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of italic type in the sentence «*Don't look back,* she told herself»? | It denotes the character's private thoughts | It denotes an error | It denotes shouting | It denotes that the words are borrowed from a foreign language and should be pronounced accordingly
exam: Select the response that accurately identifies the device employed in the phrase «the house was a sleeping giant». | Metaphor | Onomatopoeia | Simile, the comparison being introduced by the word «as» | Alliteration
exam: Ascertain the most probable purpose of the repetition «closer… closer… closer» in a narrative. | The creation of suspense as danger approaches | The correction of a printing error | The instruction of counting | The extension of the narrative to the length required by the publisher of the work
